# lattice

A knowledge base for you and your AI agents.

lattice gives your AI agents long-term memory and a library of reference they can read. It keeps your own notes and documents in one private store. And it publishes what you choose, peer-to-peer or on the open web. All of it runs on a personal server you control, so your data and your agents' memory never touch someone else's cloud.

(You may well be reading this page through lattice right now.)

## How it works

lattice runs on your own server, an Urbit ship, and serves its own web client. There is nothing to host elsewhere and nobody to sign up with. Everything below lives on that server: your documents, your agents' memory, and whatever you decide to publish.

* The server: lattice installs onto your Urbit ship, a personal server you run. Paste one command into its console and lattice is there at /apps/lattice. New versions arrive on their own.
* The client: /apps/lattice to read, /apps/lattice/app to write. On a phone, add it to your home screen: it is a full PWA that works offline.
* The surfaces: a web app, an optional desktop app for Linux and macOS, a filesystem mount that turns your pages into plain files, and plugins that give Claude Code and Hermes memory. One store behind all of them.

## Memory and knowledge for your agents

Plugins for Claude Code and Hermes turn lattice into long-term memory for your agents. At the start of a session the agent loads your standing rules and an index of what it knows. With each task it recalls what bears on the work. It saves facts as it learns them, signed with its name, and supersedes them when they change. Anything an agent saves shows up in your knowledge browser, and anything you write is there for the agent to find.

Beyond its own memory, an agent can read a wider body of reference: your personal wiki, pages that other people's servers publish, and web pages you have clipped and saved. It draws on all of that without leaving your control or going through a vendor's API. And if you run orrery, the world-model app on the same ship, lattice opens each session with orrery's read on your day and keeps your daily briefs as private journal pages.

Each agent gets its own key, made in lattice's settings and scoped to just the memory and pages you allow. Publishing, sharing, and settings stay yours, and you can revoke any key on its own. Your agents' memory lives on your server.

=> https://github.com/nisfeb/lattice/blob/main/docs/agent-knowledge.md Details, and the HTTP routes behind the plugins

## What it does

* Documents in any format: markdown, HTML, plain text, or a page written in Hoon (the server's own language) that computes its own content, with commands, state, and references to other pages.
* Private notes: a private, tagged note store with full history and a trash. Browse by folder and tag; drive it from the web app, an HTTP API, the agent plugins, or the filesystem mount.
* Search: a full-text index over your documents and notes, from the reader's search bar, every hit badged with its scope.
* Your pages as files: lattice-fs mounts every page as a local file, so grep and cat run from memory and your editor's backup files never touch the server.
* Publish on your terms: every saved page gets an address other servers can fetch directly, peer-to-peer. Pages and folders can also go public on the open web. Share a folder and you have published a site.
* Editor: highlighting for every format, live preview, inline errors for programmable pages, templates, folder-level share, move and delete, drag-and-drop upload of files or directories. A page named "My Page" keeps that name in the tree.
* Works offline: saves, deletes, moves and renames queue locally and replay in order when the connection returns; an overwritten concurrent edit is kept as a real page under conflicts/.
* Export and restore: every page and memory as one archive of plain files; restore puts them back with paths, tags and dates intact. The desktop app schedules backups to a folder on your machine.
* Sharing that scales: named groups with per-path read and edit grants, per-file grants, a banlist that revokes on ban, and comments from readers with a moderation inbox.
* Follow and subscribe: follow other servers, or subscribe to one file and get each edit as it happens over a live subscription.
* Browse the network: read what any server publishes, bookmark it, pull up a stranger's full index, or copy a remote file onto your own server.
* Light and dark, following your system.

## What it isn't

* Not a hosted service. You run it on your own server (an Urbit ship): yours, a friend's, or a hosted one.
* Not just a website, except for what you choose to publish on the open web, which your own server serves.
* Not an app to install. The web client is served by your server; the desktop app and the filesystem mount are optional extras.

## Install

Paste into your server's console:

```
|install ~ricsul-bilwyt %grubbery
```

lattice installs through grubbery, the small app platform it runs on. Approve its permissions at /apps/grubbery/permits, where it says what it needs and why. Then log into your server's web login with its access code (+code) and open /apps/lattice. New versions arrive on their own. The optional desktop app for Linux and macOS is on the releases page:

=> https://github.com/nisfeb/lattice/releases/latest  Releases
=> https://github.com/nisfeb/lattice  Source on GitHub
=> https://urbit.org/overview/running-urbit  Don't have a server yet? Boot one

